# Computed-results cache

A computed value about a specific object is stored once in `cache/` and reused. The table format and
commands are in [../../cache/manifest.md](../../cache/manifest.md). The examples on this
page use one running example: the chromatic number of a graph, keyed by the graph's graph6
string (any other object and quantity work the same way).

## Look up before computing

Before computing a value about a specific object, run `bin/cache lookup <key…>`.
- Why: recomputing a known value wastes the budget that should go to new cases, and two
  computations of one value that disagree go unnoticed unless they meet in one table.
- Not for: values that are cheaper to compute than to look up (a few milliseconds), or
  exploratory throwaway runs whose output nobody will reuse.

## Key on the canonical form

Two keys that name the same object must compare equal. Set `[cache].key_columns` and, when
objects have several spellings (a graph up to relabelling, a polynomial up to scaling), a `[cache].canonicalizer` command that returns one normal form.
- Why: without it, the same object stored under two spellings is computed twice and a
  disagreement between the two copies is never seen.
- Not for: keys that are already canonical by construction (an integer, a sorted tuple, a graph6 string already in canonical labelling).

## A conflict is a finding, not an overwrite

`bin/cache append` refuses a value that differs from one already stored for the same key and
quantity. Find out which one is right (a bug, a precision limit, a key that is not canonical
enough) before using `--force`.
- Why: silently replacing a value hides exactly the error the cache is best placed to catch.
- Not for: a value you have shown to be wrong; then `--force` and record why in the
  provenance column.

## Record where a value came from and what it cost

The `provenance` column says how the value was obtained ({{#if modules.experiments}}an experiment run bundle `experiments/<slug>/runs/<ts>/`, {{/if}}a
script and its version, a published table with page). For expensive values, also store the
cost as its own quantity (here `cost_seconds` next to `chromatic_number`, for the same key), so
a reuser can judge whether recomputing at a larger size is affordable.
- Why: a value with no source cannot be re-checked, and a reuse that silently triggers a
  much larger computation stalls the session.

{{#if modules.experiments}}
## Concurrent writers use their own table

An experiment writes its values to `experiments/<slug>/results-catalog.tsv` instead of a
shared table. Lookups take the union, so nothing is lost.
- Why: two agents appending to one file at once can corrupt it or produce merge duplicates.
- Duplicates across tables are harmless: lookups show each value once.

{{/if}}
## A cached value keeps its scope

A cached value is only as strong as the computation behind it: a value computed to finite
precision, or verified up to a bound, says so in its provenance. Reusing it does not make it
exact.
